### Rationale
I built an FP16 kernel (`tc_h`) for B > 256. It passes 16/16 workloads on the rented B200. Compared with r5 on the same GPU, the large batches (449–919) are 7–20% faster and 373/384 are unchanged (+1%). The emulator predicts a portal score of 0.6525 against 0.6256 today. The plan's option A (converter warps load W fp32 straight from global) only worked after one fix. A `release` arrive from a converter thread waits for that thread's prefetch loads still in flight, which cost about one DRAM latency per k-block. Converter warps now signal a named barrier, and a forwarder warp with no loads in flight does the release arrive. A relaxed arrive would be faster (53 vs 59 µs at 384, 79 vs 94 at 691), but it drops the memory-ordering guarantee, so I did not ship it. The kernel now waits on load latency and DRAM, not on the MMA, so it should lose less to the 1.5 GHz portal clock than r5 did. The PDL plan was blocked by the lint, so emb conversion is a separate plain launch: two launches per call for B > 256. The 131–256 band (step 5) is untested and stays on TF32 `tc_pair`.

